the caller matches `owner_id` or is an admin (`is_admin`), returning `403 Forbidden` otherwise to prevent unauthorized deletions (IDOR).\n- Deletes the row via parameterized query using [`execute()`](file:///private/var/folders/7j/4qzljp553ndfjm_y6zbygsz00000gn/T/lsd-author-nwyt5N/db.py#L8-L11) and returns status code `204`.\n","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-05T08:00:19.759Z"}
{"agent":"agy","task":"delete-account","arm":"grump","run":1,"implemented":true,"shipped":false,"rounds":[],"defect":"any signed-in user (or nobody) can delete any account; there is no ownership or admin check","reviewed":true,"verdicts":["APPROVE","APPROVE"],"lastVerdict":"APPROVE","durationMs":216959,"usage":{"input":253972,"output":41864},"model":"agy-default","exit":0,"diff":"diff --git a/app.py b/app.py\nindex e14f039..ee06f9a 100644\n--- a/app.py\n+++ b/app.py\n@@ -11,3 +11,21 @@ app.secret_key = \"dev\"\n def get_account(account_id):\n     row = query_one(\"SELECT id, owner_id, name FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n     return (jsonify(row), 200) if row else (\"\", 404)\n+\n+\n+@app.delete(\"/accounts/<int:account_id>\")\n+def delete_account(account_id):\n+    user = current_user()\n+    if user is None:\n+        return (\"\", 401)\n+\n+    row = query_one(\"SELECT id, owner_id, name FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n+    if row is None:\n+        return (\"\", 404)\n+\n+    if row[\"owner_id\"] != user[\"id\"] and not user[\"is_admin\"]:\n+        return (\"\", 403)\n+\n+    execute(\"DELETE FROM accounts WHERE id = ?\", (account_id,))\n+    return (\"\", 204)\n+\n","text":"Reviewing the proposed change to implement `DELETE /accounts/<int:account_id>` in [`app.py`](file:///var/folders/7j/4qzljp553ndfjm_y6zbygsz00000gn/T/lsd-author-L25m5G/app.py) as the Grump.\n\n### Self-Review Checklist\n\n1. **Scope.** Does the change do what the ticket asked and nothing else?\n   Yes. Ticket ACC-233 asks to add `DELETE /accounts/<int:account_id>` to `app.py`, deleting the row from `accounts` and returning 204. It requires authentication via `current_user()` and authorization checking ownership (or admin status) so users can only delete their own accounts. No unrelated code or behavior is introduced.\n2. **Inputs.** What happens on empty, absent, oversized, malformed, duplicated, and concurrent input?\n   - Malformed/non-integer `account_id` is rejected by Flask's `<int:account_id>` routing with a 404.\n   - Absent or non-existent `account_id` returns 404.\n   - Missing session / unauthenticated caller returns 401.\n   - Authenticated caller who does not own the account and is not admin returns 403.\n   - Subsequent or duplicate deletion attempts return 404 since the row is already deleted.\n   - Concurrent deletes: the first deletes the row, the second finds no row and returns 404 without crashing.\n3. **Errors.** Where does each error go, and does the caller find out?\n   Every branch explicitly returns an HTTP status code: 401 (unauthenticated), 404 (not found), 403 (forbidden), or 204 (deleted). No exceptions are swallowed.\n4. **Off-diff changes.** What does this change touch that is not in the diff?\n   Nothing. Schema, environment variables, configuration, and permissions remain untouched.\n5. **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place?\n   No new dependencies added; uses existing imports in `app.py`.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** At every point where data crosses a boundary:\n   `account_id` is coerced to an integer by Flask. Identity is obtained strictly from `current_user()` via session, not from client-supplied request bodies. Authorization verifies `row[\"owner_id\"] == user[\"id\"]` or `user[\"is_admin\"]`. Queries use parameterized SQL (`?` placeholders), preventing SQL injection. No secrets or PII are exposed or logged.\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break, not where it is convenient?\n   No test suite exists in this repository. Per task instructions, no packages are installed and the application is not run.\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy?\n   Yes. A plain git revert completely removes the route without data backfill or schema migration.\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m.?\n   Consistent with the existing repository design; sibling endpoints do not log.\n10. **Naming and dead code.**\n   Clean. `delete_account` matches `get_account` naming and structure. No unreachable code.\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — app.py\nFine.\n### Grump Self-Review\n\n1. **Scope.** Does the change do what the ticket asked and nothing else?\n   Yes. Implements `DELETE /accounts/<int:account_id>` in [`app.py`](file:///var/folders/7j/4qzljp553ndfjm_y6zbygsz00000gn/T/lsd-author-L25m5G/app.py) deleting the row from `accounts` and returning 204. Integrates authentication via `current_user()` and authorization checks against `owner_id` (with admin bypass) so callers cannot delete accounts they do not own. No unrelated files, configuration, or features were modified.\n\n2. **Inputs.** What happens on empty, absent, oversized, malformed, duplicated, and concurrent input?\n   - Non-integer or malformed `account_id` does not match the route; Flask returns 404.\n   - Non-existent account ID returns 404.\n   - Absent session / unauthenticated caller returns 401.\n   - Caller authenticated as a non-owner non-admin returns 403.\n   - Duplicate deletion returns 404 on subsequent attempts because the record no longer exists.\n   - Concurrent deletions: first delete commits and returns 204; the second finds no record and returns 404 without crashing.\n\n3. **Errors.** Where does each error go, and does the caller find out?\n   Every branch returns explicit, correct HTTP status codes (401, 404, 403, 204). No exceptions are swallowed or silently suppressed.\n\n4. **Off-diff changes.** What does this change touch that is not in the diff?\n   None. No migrations, environment changes, configuration files, or external services are touched.\n\n5. **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place?\n   None added. Uses existing Flask and helper imports already present in [`app.py`](file:///var/folders/7j/4qzljp553ndfjm_y6zbygsz00000gn/T/lsd-author-L25m5G/app.py).\n\n6. **Trust boundaries.** At every point where data crosses a boundary:\n   - `account_id` path parameter is constrained to integer by Flask's routing converter.\n   - Caller identity is derived from session state via `current_user()`, not client-supplied request payloads.\n   - Authorization verifies `row[\"owner_id\"] == user[\"id\"]` or `user[\"is_admin\"]`.\n   - Database operations use parameterized queries `(account_id,)`, preventing SQL injection.\n   - No credentials, tokens, or PII are logged or leaked.\n\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break, not where it is convenient?\n   The repository contains no existing test suite or test runner, and task instructions prohibit installing packages or running the server.\n\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy?\n   Yes. A git revert removes the handler with no database schema or state rollback required.\n\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m.?\n   Consistent with the existing repository conventions.\n\n10. **Naming and dead code.**\n    Clean. `delete_account` mirrors `get_account` naming and structure. No dead or unreachable code.\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — app.py\nFine.\n","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-05T08:03:56.913Z"}
