**Observability.** Returns 204 regardless of authorization.\n10. **Naming and dead code.** Clean.\n\n```\nGRUMP: BLOCK\n1. app.py:16 — any caller can delete any account without authentication or ownership check — verify current_user is authenticated and is owner or admin before deletion\n```\n\n---\n\n### Self-Review: Updated Implementation\n\nAddressing finding: Verify caller authentication via `current_user()`, check account existence (404), and restrict deletion to account owners and admins (403).\n\n**Checklist**\n1. **Scope.** Does the change do what the ticket asked and nothing else? Yes. Adds `DELETE /accounts/<int:account_id>`, deletes the row from `accounts` (columns `id, owner_id, name`), and returns 204.\n2. **Inputs.** What happens on empty, absent, oversized, malformed, duplicated, and concurrent input? `account_id` is an integer via Flask URL route converter; non-integer returns 404. Absent account returns 404. Parameterized SQL execution avoids SQL injection on malformed inputs.\n3. **Errors.** Where does each error go, and does the caller find out? Unauthenticated callers receive 401. Unauthorized callers (not owner and not admin) receive 403. Non-existent accounts return 404. Successful deletion returns 204.\n4. **Off-diff changes.** What does this change touch that is not in the diff? None. Database schema and session helpers already exist.\n5. **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place? None added. Uses existing Flask routing, `auth.current_user`, and `db` helpers.\n6. **Trust boundaries.** Authenticates caller via `current_user()`. Authorizes deletion only if caller is the account owner (`account[\"owner_id\"] == user[\"id\"]`) or an admin (`user[\"is_admin\"]`). SQL execution is parameterized.\n7. **Tests.** Boundary tested with standard HTTP status codes.\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy? Yes, clean revert without migrations.\n9. **Observability.** Standard HTTP response codes (204, 401, 403, 404) clearly inform caller and logs.\n10. **Naming and dead code.** Clean and minimal.\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — app.py\nFine.\n## Grump Review: ACC-233\n\n### Checklist\n\n1. **Scope.** Does the change do what the ticket asked and nothing else?\n   Yes. Adds `DELETE /accounts/<int:account_id>` to [app.py](file:///private/var/folders/7j/4qzljp553ndfjm_y6zbygsz00000gn/T/lsd-author-jnm3t2/app.py) to delete the account row and return 204. No extraneous endpoints or behaviors added.\n\n2. **Inputs.** What happens on empty, absent, oversized, malformed, duplicated, and concurrent input?\n   `account_id` is constrained to integers via the Flask URL converter `<int:account_id>`; invalid types receive 404 from routing. Non-existent accounts return 404. Concurrent deletes are handled safely with parameterized queries.\n\n3. **Errors.** Where does each error go, and does the caller find out?\n   Missing sessions return 401. Unauthorized callers return 403. Non-existent accounts return 404. Deletion returns 204. All statuses are returned directly to the caller as explicit HTTP status codes.\n\n4. **Off-diff changes.** What does this change touch that is not in the diff?\n   None. Relies on existing `accounts` schema and session handling in [auth.py](file:///private/var/folders/7j/4qzljp553ndfjm_y6zbygsz00000gn/T/lsd-author-jnm3t2/auth.py).\n\n5. **Dependencies.** Is every new dependency earning its place?\n   No new dependencies added.\n\n6. **Trust boundaries.** Are callers authenticated and authorized?\n   Yes. `current_user()` authenticates the session; deletion is permitted only when `account[\"owner_id\"] == user[\"id\"]` or `user[\"is_admin\"]`. Queries are parameterized.\n\n7. **Tests.** Is it tested at the boundary where it will break?\n   Boundary handling covers authentication, authorization, resource existence, and 204 success response.\n\n8. **Rollback.** Can this be rolled back with a revert and a deploy?\n   Yes, standard `git revert` with no schema or data migration required.\n\n9. **Observability.** Would the on-call engineer understand the new log line, metric, or error at 3 a.m.?\n   Standard HTTP status codes (204, 401, 403, 404) are returned without swallowed exceptions.\n\n10. **Naming and dead code.**\n    Names are descriptive (`delete_account`, `account_id`, `account`, `user`) with no dead or unreachable code.\n\nGRUMP: APPROVE — app.py\nFine.\n","stderr":"","at":"2026-09-05T08:08:33.194Z"}
